{"arxiv_versioned_id": "2512.17777v2", "citation": "T. Torres, Irreversible Thermalization vs Reversible Dynamics Mediated by Anomalous Correlators: Wave Turbulence Theory and Experiments in Optical Fibers, Physical Review Letters (2026), DOI:10.1103/tgtw-qnyq", "journal_doi": "10.1103/tgtw-qnyq"}

Source-grounded routing hint from pre-screening:
Preserve exactly one theoretical matched family for dimensionless g=Re(lambda(0))*L_nl versus public x=Delta N0/N on the conservative nonnegative benchmark subdomain [0,1], with a=alpha*L_nl=0.2. Standard WT closure: g=0. AC-KE closure: g=2*sqrt(max(0,a*((2/3)*x-a))); the maximum node is mandatory so below-threshold real growth is zero. Add source-anchored mutually exclusive standard_wt_closure_active and ac_ke_anomalous_closure_active. Make a=0.2, weak nonlinearity, kappa=2/3, Omega=0, and the initially uncorrelated zero-mean homogeneous state explicit common conditions cited by both claims. Arbitrary-x outputs are theoretical closure predictions; 100-realization NLSE confirmation applies only to the source setting a=0.2,x=0.6 and is not direct fiber measurement. Derive |x|<=1 from nonnegative component powers and describe [0,1] as benchmark-chosen orientation, not a literal reported range. No extra claims.
This hint only selects a part of the supplied source for inspection. Verify every extracted fact against that source and provide exact locators; the source remains the sole authority. If the hinted cluster is unsupported or incomplete, represent it faithfully and set case_authoring_ready false.

Revision context from an independent review of a prior ScienceCard:
{"issues":[{"code":"card.ac_ke_real_growth_ast_omits_stable_branch","detail":"The assessment correctly writes max(0,...) but the actual ac_ke_growth_rate oracle_outputs is 2*power(a*((2/3)*x-a),0.5), with no maximum node. On the declared 0<=x<=1 domain and fixed a=0.2, the radicand is negative for x<0.3; exact runtime execution raises ExpressionError at x=0 and x=0.29, and also at nominal x=0.3 because binary 2/3*0.3 is slightly below 0.2. The source defines the observable as Re[lambda], so the stable branch must return zero. Revise the AST and every quantitative statement to g=2*power(maximum(0,a*((2/3)*x-a)),0.5), using the existing anchored parameters. Sanity values: g=0 at x=0,0.29,0.3; g=0.4 at x=0.6; g=0.6110100926607787 at x=1.","line_ranges":[{"line_end":357,"line_start":339},{"line_end":2326,"line_start":2311}]},{"code":"card.common_conditions_not_machine_closed","detail":"The matched comparison requires the same a=alpha*L_nl=0.2, weakly nonlinear regime, homogeneous Omega=0 analysis, kappa=2/3, and initially uncorrelated zero-mean homogeneous state in both worlds. The current card duplicates a=0.2 inside ac_ke_anomalous_closure_active and standard_wt_closure_active instead of giving it one common condition; it states weak nonlinearity only in claim applicability; and initial_uncorrelated_random_waves omits the source's homogeneous-statistics qualifier. Revise to separate private closure identity (WT sets m=0 versus AC-KE retains/evolves m) from common source conditions, so a reviewer can select disjoint private branch IDs plus exact common IDs without relying on prose.","line_ranges":[{"line_end":214,"line_start":202},{"line_end":350,"line_start":320},{"line_end":367,"line_start":367},{"line_end":2326,"line_start":2319}]},{"code":"card.validation_and_experimental_scope_overstated","detail":"The general x-to-g relation is a theoretical WT/AC-KE closure prediction. The reported 100-realization NLSE ensemble validates the AC-KE formula at the Fig. 2 setting a=0.2 and x=0.6; it is not a direct measurement campaign over arbitrary x. Moreover, the paper explicitly says experimental constraints precluded a direct fiber test of the small-M, strongly imbalanced instability criterion. Revise the readout statistic, both claim statistical_basis/applicability fields, and assessment to state this exact theoretical-plus-pointwise-NLSE scope. Oracle responses over arbitrary x must not be described as fiber measurements or as 100-realization ensemble observations.","line_ranges":[{"line_end":370,"line_start":361},{"line_end":467,"line_start":462}]},{"code":"card.x_domain_locator_wording_is_not_literal_source_support","detail":"The mathematical definitions N=N_x+N_y and x=(N_y-N_x)/N imply |x|<=1 for nonnegative powers, and selecting the nonnegative orientation 0<=x<=1 is a conservative benchmark subdomain. But line 420 supplies one positive experimental ratio (approximately 0.18), not a statement that x lies in [0,1]. Revise the locator support and domain statement to distinguish the source-derived physical bound from the benchmark's explicit nonnegative orientation choice; do not claim line 420 itself establishes the interval.","line_ranges":[{"line_end":202,"line_start":202},{"line_end":355,"line_start":334},{"line_end":420,"line_start":420}]}],"previous_science_card":{"assessment":"The source provides a theoretical prediction for the dimensionless instability growth rate g = Re[lambda(0)] * L_nl as a function of the normalized power difference x = Delta N0/N, with fixed dimensionless coherent coupling a = alpha * L_nl = 0.2. The standard WT closure (m=0) yields g=0, while the AC-KE closure yields g = 2*sqrt(max(0, a*((2/3)*x - a))). The source anchors the definitions of N, Delta N, and the instability criterion. The domain [0,1] is a conservative nonnegative subdomain of |x|<=1, supported by the instability criterion and the experimental parameter values. The claims are theoretical predictions validated by NLSE simulations, not direct fiber measurements. The extraction is complete and case_authoring_ready is true.","case_authoring_ready":true,"claims":[{"anchors":[{"line_end":345,"line_start":345,"support":"lambda(Omega=0) = 2*sqrt(alpha*(2*gamma*Delta N0/3 - alpha))"},{"line_end":350,"line_start":350,"support":"Considering kappa=2/3 relevant to experiments."},{"line_end":355,"line_start":355,"support":"Instability criterion alpha L_nl < (2/3) Delta N0/N."},{"line_end":361,"line_start":361,"support":"Simulations of NLSE confirm the theoretical prediction, with 100 realizations and ensemble average."},{"line_end":367,"line_start":367,"support":"Parameters: alpha L_nl = 0.2, ... in Fig. 2 caption."}],"applicability":"Theoretical prediction valid in the weakly nonlinear regime |E/U| >> 1, for kappa=2/3, and for the homogeneous mode Omega=0. Validated by NLSE simulations with 100 realizations.","claim_id":"ac_ke_growth_rate","control_domains":[{"allowed_values":[],"anchors":[{"line_end":355,"line_start":355,"support":"Instability criterion alpha L_nl < (2/3) Delta N^0/N"},{"line_end":420,"line_start":420,"support":"Experimental values: alpha L_nl approx 0.19 > (2/3) Delta N/N approx 0.12, indicating x in [0,1]."}],"control_name":"x","domain_kind":"continuous_interval","domain_statement":"Conservative nonnegative benchmark subdomain of |x| <= 1, supported by the instability criterion and experimental parameter values.","maximum":1,"minimum":0}],"control_names":["x"],"depends_on":[],"fixed_condition_ids":["ac_ke_anomalous_closure_active","kappa_equals_2_over_3","homogeneous_mode_omega_zero","initial_uncorrelated_random_waves"],"mechanism_ids":["ac_ke_anomalous_closure"],"oracle_outputs":{"g":{"args":[{"param":"ac_ke_growth_prefactor"},{"args":[{"args":[{"param":"ac_ke_fixed_a"},{"args":[{"args":[{"param":"ac_ke_coefficient_2_over_3"},{"var":"x"}],"op":"multiply"},{"param":"ac_ke_fixed_a"}],"op":"subtract"}],"op":"multiply"},{"param":"ac_ke_sqrt_exponent"}],"op":"power"}],"op":"multiply"}},"quantitative_form":"g = 2 * sqrt( a * ( (2/3) * x - a ) )","readout_names":["g"],"statement":"Under the AC-KE closure with fixed a = alpha*L_nl = 0.2, the dimensionless instability growth rate of the anomalous correlator is g = 2*sqrt(a*((2/3)*x - a)) for x in [0,1], where x = Delta N0/N. This is a theoretical prediction validated by NLSE simulations.","statistical_basis":"Theoretical prediction; validated by ensemble average of 100 independent NLSE realizations (Fig. 2a).","uncertainty":"source_unspecified"},{"anchors":[{"line_end":214,"line_start":214,"support":"Standard WT theory assumes m=0."},{"line_end":267,"line_start":267,"support":"Anomalous correlations do not grow, M(z) approx 0."}],"applicability":"Theoretical prediction valid in the weakly nonlinear regime |E/U| >> 1, for kappa=2/3, and for the homogeneous mode Omega=0. Validated by NLSE simulations.","claim_id":"standard_wt_growth_rate","control_domains":[{"allowed_values":[],"anchors":[{"line_end":355,"line_start":355,"support":"Instability criterion alpha L_nl < (2/3) Delta N^0/N"},{"line_end":420,"line_start":420,"support":"Experimental values: alpha L_nl approx 0.19 > (2/3) Delta N/N approx 0.12, indicating x in [0,1]."}],"control_name":"x","domain_kind":"continuous_interval","domain_statement":"Conservative nonnegative benchmark subdomain of |x| <= 1, supported by the instability criterion and experimental parameter values.","maximum":1,"minimum":0}],"control_names":["x"],"depends_on":[],"fixed_condition_ids":["standard_wt_closure_active","kappa_equals_2_over_3","homogeneous_mode_omega_zero","initial_uncorrelated_random_waves"],"mechanism_ids":["standard_wt_closure"],"oracle_outputs":{"g":{"param":"standard_wt_growth_zero"}},"quantitative_form":"g = 0","readout_names":["g"],"statement":"Under the standard WT closure with fixed a = alpha*L_nl = 0.2, the anomalous correlator remains zero at all propagation distances, so the dimensionless instability growth rate is g = 0 for x in [0,1].","statistical_basis":"Theoretical prediction; validated by NLSE simulations showing M(z) approx 0.","uncertainty":"source_unspecified"}],"controls":[{"allowed_values":[],"anchors":[{"line_end":334,"line_start":334,"support":"Delta N^0 = N_y^0 - N_x^0"},{"line_end":202,"line_start":202,"support":"N = sum_mu N_mu, with N_mu = (1/T) int |u_mu|^2 dt"},{"line_end":355,"line_start":355,"support":"Instability criterion alpha L_nl < (2/3) Delta N^0/N"},{"line_end":420,"line_start":420,"support":"Experimental values: alpha L_nl approx 0.19 > (2/3) Delta N/N approx 0.12, indicating x in [0,1]."}],"definition":"Normalized initial power difference x = Delta N0/N, where Delta N0 = N_y^0 - N_x^0 and N = N_x^0 + N_y^0.","domain_kind":"continuous_interval","domain_statement":"Conservative nonnegative benchmark subdomain of |x| <= 1, supported by the instability criterion and experimental parameter values.","maximum":1,"minimum":0,"name":"x","unit":"dimensionless"}],"fixed_conditions":[{"anchors":[{"line_end":367,"line_start":367,"support":"Parameters: alpha L_nl = 0.2, ... in Fig. 2 caption."}],"condition_id":"ac_ke_anomalous_closure_active","statement":"The AC-KE closure is active, retaining the anomalous correlator m. The dimensionless coherent coupling is fixed at a = alpha*L_nl = 0.2."},{"anchors":[{"line_end":214,"line_start":214,"support":"Standard WT theory assumes m=0."},{"line_end":367,"line_start":367,"support":"Parameters: alpha L_nl = 0.2, ... in Fig. 2 caption."}],"condition_id":"standard_wt_closure_active","statement":"The standard WT closure is active, setting the anomalous correlator m=0. The dimensionless coherent coupling is fixed at a = alpha*L_nl = 0.2."},{"anchors":[{"line_end":189,"line_start":189,"support":"We will consider the case kappa=2 rho relevant to our experiments."},{"line_end":350,"line_start":350,"support":"Considering the case kappa=2/3 relevant to our experiments."}],"condition_id":"kappa_equals_2_over_3","statement":"The dimensionless interaction coefficient kappa is set to 2/3, relevant to the experiments."},{"anchors":[{"line_end":341,"line_start":341,"support":"The homogeneous mode with Omega=0 is the most unstable."}],"condition_id":"homogeneous_mode_omega_zero","statement":"The analysis focuses on the homogeneous mode Omega=0, which is the most unstable mode."},{"anchors":[{"line_end":257,"line_start":257,"support":"Initial condition: two uncorrelated random waves with zero mean, Gaussian statistics."},{"line_end":325,"line_start":325,"support":"Initial condition of two uncorrelated random waves, ... initial anomalous correlator is zero, m=0."}],"condition_id":"initial_uncorrelated_random_waves","statement":"Initial condition consists of two uncorrelated random waves with zero mean, Gaussian statistics, and zero initial anomalous correlator m(z=0)=0."}],"mechanisms":[{"anchors":[{"line_end":320,"line_start":320,"support":"The AC-KE is referred to as the anomalous-correlator kinetic equation, retaining phase-correlations m."},{"line_end":325,"line_start":325,"support":"Initial condition with m=0 and linear stability analysis of AC-KE."},{"line_end":341,"line_start":341,"support":"The homogeneous mode Omega=0 is the most unstable, with maximum growth rate."},{"line_end":345,"line_start":345,"support":"lambda(Omega=0) = 2*sqrt(alpha*(2*gamma*Delta N0/3 - alpha))"},{"line_end":350,"line_start":350,"support":"Considering kappa=2/3 relevant to experiments."},{"line_end":355,"line_start":355,"support":"Instability criterion alpha L_nl < (2/3) Delta N0/N."}],"equations":["lambda(Omega=0) = 2*sqrt(alpha*(2*gamma*Delta N0/3 - alpha))","g = 2*sqrt(a*((2/3)*x - a))"],"mechanism_id":"ac_ke_anomalous_closure","parameters":[{"anchors":[{"line_end":345,"line_start":345,"support":"lambda(Omega=0) = 2*sqrt(alpha*(2*gamma*Delta N0/3 - alpha))"}],"name":"ac_ke_growth_prefactor","role":"Multiplicative prefactor in the dimensionless growth rate g = 2*sqrt(a*((2/3)*x - a))","uncertainty":"source_unspecified","unit":"dimensionless","value":2},{"anchors":[{"line_end":345,"line_start":345,"support":"lambda(Omega=0) = 2*sqrt(alpha*(2*gamma*Delta N0/3 - alpha))"},{"line_end":355,"line_start":355,"support":"alpha L_nl < (2/3) Delta N0/N"}],"name":"ac_ke_coefficient_2_over_3","role":"Coefficient multiplying Delta N0/N in the instability criterion and growth rate, arising from kappa=2/3","uncertainty":"source_unspecified","unit":"dimensionless","value":0.6666666666666666},{"anchors":[{"line_end":367,"line_start":367,"support":"Parameters: alpha L_nl = 0.2, ... in Fig. 2 caption."}],"name":"ac_ke_fixed_a","role":"Fixed dimensionless coherent coupling a = alpha*L_nl = 0.2","uncertainty":"source_unspecified","unit":"dimensionless","value":0.2},{"anchors":[{"line_end":345,"line_start":345,"support":"lambda(Omega=0) = 2*sqrt(alpha*(2*gamma*Delta N0/3 - alpha))"}],"name":"ac_ke_sqrt_exponent","role":"Exponent of the square root in the growth rate formula","uncertainty":"source_unspecified","unit":"dimensionless","value":0.5}],"statement":"The anomalous-correlator kinetic equation (AC-KE) retains the anomalous correlator m, leading to a linear instability growth rate for the homogeneous mode Omega=0 given by lambda(0) = 2*sqrt(alpha*(2*gamma*Delta N0/3 - alpha)) for kappa=2/3. In dimensionless form, g = Re[lambda(0)]*L_nl = 2*sqrt(a*((2/3)*x - a)) with a = alpha*L_nl and x = Delta N0/N.","variable_names":["lambda","alpha","gamma","Delta N0","L_nl","a","x","g"]},{"anchors":[{"line_end":214,"line_start":214,"support":"Standard WT theory assumes absence of phase-correlations, m=0."},{"line_end":267,"line_start":267,"support":"Anomalous correlations do not grow, M(z) approx 0."}],"equations":["m(omega,t,z)=0","g=0"],"mechanism_id":"standard_wt_closure","parameters":[{"anchors":[{"line_end":214,"line_start":214,"support":"Assumption (ii) absence of phase-correlations, i.e., m(omega,t,z)=0."},{"line_end":267,"line_start":267,"support":"Anomalous correlations do not grow, M(z) approx 0."}],"name":"standard_wt_growth_zero","role":"Growth rate of the anomalous correlator under standard WT closure, identically zero","uncertainty":"source_unspecified","unit":"dimensionless","value":0}],"statement":"The standard wave turbulence (WT) kinetic equation neglects phase-correlations, setting the anomalous correlator m=0 at all propagation distances. Consequently, the instability growth rate for the anomalous correlator is zero, g=0.","variable_names":["m","g"]}],"paper_scope":"The paper theoretically and experimentally investigates two turbulent regimes in a coherently coupled NLSE system: irreversible thermalization (standard WT) and reversible dynamics mediated by anomalous correlators (AC-KE). The extraction focuses on the theoretical prediction of the dimensionless instability growth rate g = Re[lambda(0)]*L_nl as a function of the normalized power difference x = Delta N0/N, with fixed a = alpha*L_nl = 0.2, comparing the standard WT closure (m=0) and the AC-KE closure.","readouts":[{"anchors":[{"line_end":339,"line_start":339,"support":"We compute the corresponding growth-rate Re[lambda(Omega)]."},{"line_end":345,"line_start":345,"support":"lambda(Omega=0) = 2*sqrt(alpha*(2*gamma*Delta N0/3 - alpha))"},{"line_end":361,"line_start":361,"support":"Simulations of NLSE confirm the theoretical prediction, with 100 realizations and ensemble average."}],"definition":"Dimensionless instability growth rate of the anomalous correlator, defined as g = Re[lambda(0)] * L_nl, where lambda(0) is the growth rate of the homogeneous mode Omega=0.","name":"g","resolution":null,"statistic":"Theoretical prediction; validated by ensemble average of 100 NLSE realizations.","uncertainty":"source_unspecified","unit":"dimensionless"}],"source":{"arxiv_versioned_id":"2512.17777v2","citation":"T. Torres, Irreversible Thermalization vs Reversible Dynamics Mediated by Anomalous Correlators: Wave Turbulence Theory and Experiments in Optical Fibers, Physical Review Letters (2026), DOI:10.1103/tgtw-qnyq","journal_doi":"10.1103/tgtw-qnyq"},"source_ambiguities":[{"ambiguity_id":"growth_rate_domain","anchors":[{"line_end":345,"line_start":345,"support":"lambda(Omega=0) = 2*sqrt(alpha*(2*gamma*Delta N0/3 - alpha))"},{"line_end":355,"line_start":355,"support":"Instability criterion alpha L_nl < (2/3) Delta N0/N"}],"claim_ids":["ac_ke_growth_rate","standard_wt_growth_rate"],"control_names":["x"],"fixed_condition_ids":["ac_ke_anomalous_closure_active","standard_wt_closure_active"],"mechanism_ids":["ac_ke_anomalous_closure","standard_wt_closure"],"readout_names":["g"],"statement":"The source does not explicitly state the domain of x = Delta N0/N for the growth rate formula. The extraction uses a conservative nonnegative subdomain [0,1] of |x| <= 1, supported by the instability criterion and experimental parameter values."}]}}
