L000289	\noindent
L000290	{\it Reversible dynamics mediated by phase-correlations.}
L000291	
L000292	We now show that the thermalization process can be inhibited by the spontaneous emergence of phase-correlations. To clarify this regime in a general framework, we do not implicitly assume the waves to obey homogeneous statistics, i.e., we leave $n_{\mu}(\omega,t,z)$ and $m(\omega,t,z)$ to depend on a slow non-homogeneous variation in the $t$ variable.
L000293	
L000294	Starting from the NLSE (\ref{eq:nls}), we derive in the Supplementary Mayerial  the equations governing the coupled evolution of the normal and anomalous correlators \cite{supplement}:
L000295	\begin{widetext}
L000296	\begin{align}
L000297	\partial_z n_x(\omega,t) =& - 2\beta \omega \partial_t n_x
L000298	+ \gamma \partial_t \big(2 N_x(t) + \kappa N_y(t) \big)\partial_\omega n_x
L000299	+2\gamma\kappa \partial_t M^r(t) \partial_\omega m^r
L000300	+4 \gamma\kappa M^r(t) m^i  ,
L000301	\label{eq:nx_vl} \\
L000302	
L000303	
L000304	
L000305	
L000306	\partial_z m(\omega,t) =& -2\beta \omega \partial_t m
L000307	+\gamma(1+\kappa/2) \partial_t \big( N_x(t)+N_y(t) \big) \partial_\omega m
L000308	+\gamma\kappa \partial_t M^r(t)\partial_\omega \big( n_x+n_y \big)
L000309	\nonumber \\
L000310	&-i m \Big( \gamma(2-\kappa)\big(N_y(t)-N_x(t)\big)-2\alpha   \Big)
L000311	- 2i\gamma\kappa \big( n_x - n_y \big) M^r(t)   ,
L000312	\label{eq:m_vl}
L000313	\end{align}
L000314	\end{widetext}
L000315	with ${M}(t,z)=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int m(\omega,t,z)d\omega$, ${N}_{\mu}(t,z)=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int n_{\mu}(\omega,t,z)d\omega$, while the superscripts $m^{r,i}$ ($M^{r,i}$) denote the real and imaginary parts of $m$ ($M$).
L000316	The equation for $n_y$ follows from Eq.(\ref{eq:nx_vl}) by exchanging $x\leftrightarrow y$ and reversing the sign of the last term.
L000317	
L000318	The first two terms on the right-hand side of Eq.(\ref{eq:nx_vl})
L000319	
L000320	correspond to the well-established Vlasov coupling between two {\it uncorrelated} random waves. Here, the generalized Vlasov-like Eqs.(\ref{eq:nx_vl}-\ref{eq:m_vl}) provide an original extension accounting for phase-correlations, $m(\omega,t)$. In contrast to the KE (\ref{eq:wtkin}), the system (\ref{eq:nx_vl}-\ref{eq:m_vl}) is formally reversible in `time' $z$ \cite{supplement}. To avoid confusion with the usual KE~(\ref{eq:wtkin}), we shall refer to Eqs.(\ref{eq:nx_vl}-\ref{eq:m_vl}) (and Eq.(\ref{eq:s_dyn}) below) as the anomalous-correlator kinetic equation (AC-KE).
L000321	
L000322	
L000323	
L000324	
L000325	We consider an initial condition of two uncorrelated random waves, of zero mean, with homogeneous statistics and spectra $n_{\mu}(\omega,z=0)=n_{\mu}^0(\omega)$, so that the initial anomalous correlator is zero, $m(\omega,t,z=0)=0$. We carry out a linear stability analysis of Eqs.(\ref{eq:nx_vl}-\ref{eq:m_vl}) around this state. Using the Laplace-Fourier transform ${\hat m}(\omega, \Omega,\lambda)=\int_0^z dz \int m(\omega,t,z)\exp(-\lambda z-i\Omega t) dt$, we obtain the dispersion relation
L000326	$\lambda(\Omega)$ of the anomalous correlator \cite{supplement}:
L000327	\begin{eqnarray*}
L000328	
L000329	
L000330	\frac{2\pi}{\gamma\kappa} = \sum_{s} \int
L000331	\frac{ n_x^0(\omega)-n_y^0(\omega)-s\Omega \partial_\omega\big(n_x^{0}(\omega)+n_y^{0}(\omega)\big)/2}{is \lambda-2s\beta\omega \Omega - \gamma(2-\kappa)\Delta N^0+2\alpha} d\omega  ,
L000332	
L000333	\end{eqnarray*}
L000334	where $\Delta N^0=N_y^0-N_x^0$, and $s=\pm 1$.
L000335	
L000336	Assuming the initial spectra Gaussian-shaped,
L000337	
L000338	
L000339	we compute the corresponding growth-rate ${\rm Re}[\lambda(\Omega)]$.
L000340	
L000341	The analysis reveals that, in general, the homogeneous mode with $\Omega=0$ is the most unstable, with the maximum growth rate of the anomalous correlator:
L000342	
L000343	
L000344	\begin{align}
L000345	\lambda(\Omega=0)=  2\sqrt{\alpha(2 \gamma \Delta N^0/3-\alpha )},
L000346	
L000347	
L000348	\label{eq:lambda_0}
L000349	\end{align}
L000350	where we have considered the case $\kappa=2/3$ relevant to our experiments.
L000351	
L000352	
L000353	The instability criterion then reads
L000354	\begin{align}
L000355	\alpha L_{nl} < (2/3) \Delta N^0/N.
L000356	\label{eq:threshold}
L000357	\end{align}
L000358	
L000359	
